\section{Shapes Carry More Information Than Element Types}

Array and tensor languages apply operations to whole collections. Their compiler must distinguish rank, extent, layout, broadcast, reduction axes, and element type. This representative NumPy-style function has a matrix multiply followed by row-wise bias broadcast and reduction:

\begin{verbatim}
def score(x, w, b):
    y = x @ w + b
    return y.sum(axis=1)
# x:[N,K], w:[K,M], b:[M] -> result:[N]
\end{verbatim}

The annotation describes constraints rather than four concrete buffers. Matrix multiplication requires the two \(K\) extents to agree; broadcasting aligns \(b\)'s \(M\) with the last result dimension; reduction removes axis 1. Kienhuis's MatParser illustrates a related but distinct array-dataflow problem: deriving exact inter-iteration dependences for affine nested loops and converting them toward single assignment. Shape inference says which values fit; dependence analysis says which indexed computations constrain one another.

\section{Use Shape Constraints and Index Maps in the IR}

A shape-aware IR gives each tensor a ranked or unranked type, symbolic dimensions, element type, and layout. Operations carry affine or otherwise explicit index maps, broadcast dimensions, reduction identities, and alias/effect information. Views should not be confused with copies. The solver may prove \(K_x=K_w\) while leaving \(N\) and \(M\) dynamic; unresolved equalities become runtime guards.

\Cref{fig:visual-371} shows the logical flow without committing to row-major storage or a particular loop nest.

