Output artifacts can include residual shape guards, a normalized tensor graph, optimized kernels, memory plans, and target binaries. The artifact should preserve a mapping from a failed guard to the operand and source dimension that introduced it, rather than reporting only a low-level loop bound mismatch.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Infer, Specialize, and Guard a Tensor Graph}

\begin{verbatim}
constraints := collect_rank_extent_and_broadcast_constraints(graph)
solution, residual := solve_symbolic_equalities(constraints)
rewrite tensor types with every proved dimension
for unresolved requirement in residual:
    insert checked runtime guard before dependent operation
choose layouts and lower index maps to loops or library kernels
validate guards dominate all shape-dependent memory accesses
\end{verbatim}

Fusion of the bias add into matmul or reduction can avoid an intermediate tensor when index maps compose and numerical/effect rules permit it. Tiling, vectorization, and layout conversion are legal when they cover every logical index exactly as required and preserve aliasing. Reduction reassociation is not exact for floating point unless allowed. Broadcasting must not become an allocating copy unless allocation behavior is unobservable or specified accordingly.

Validation checks symbolic constraints, guard dominance, index-map bounds, and memory-plan noninterference. Differential tests include zero extents, singleton broadcast dimensions, dynamic mismatches, noncontiguous views, NaNs, and aliasing inputs. For affine loop lowering, compare the derived producer for each consumed index, echoing array dataflow analysis rather than testing only final sizes. Preservation covers values, shapes, errors, and effects; equal tensor shapes alone are insufficient.

\section{Write the Broadcast and Reduction as Index Maps}

In the \texttt{score} example, promotion makes \texttt{b} a logical \(N\)-by-\(M\) view without copying its contents. The output index \((i,j)\) of the broadcast maps to bias index \(j\), independently of \(i\). Reducing over axis one then keeps \(i\) and visits all \(j\). Confusing which coordinate the bias selects can survive shape checks when \(N=M\); use a rectangular fixture with distinct bias values.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Expression & Indices read & Shape \\
\hline
Contraction at \((i,j)\) & \(x[i,k], w[k,j]\) for each \(k\) & \(N,M\) \\
Bias at \((i,j)\) & \(b[j]\) & \(N,M\) view \\
Output at \(i\) & \(y[i,j]\) for each \(j\) & \(N\) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{verbatim}
require columns(x) == rows(w)
require length(b) == columns(w)
for i in 0 .. N-1:
    acc := reduction_identity
    for j in 0 .. M-1:
        dot := dot_identity
        for k in 0 .. K-1:
            dot := numeric_add(dot, numeric_mul(x[i,k], w[k,j]))
        acc := numeric_add(acc, numeric_add(dot, b[j]))
    result[i] := acc
\end{verbatim}

This is a fused loop model with explicit accumulation order; it implements a source that allows those operations and that order, not every library's contraction precision rules. For an integer fixture choose \(N=2,K=1,M=3\), with \(x=[2,4]\) as a column, \(w=[1,3,5]\) as a row, and \(b=[1,2,4]\). The results are \(25,43\). Also try \(K=0\) where zero-length contractions use the additive identity; the bias still contributes. Keeping guards outside the loops ensures an incompatible shape is diagnosed even when \(N=0\) and no result element is visited.

\paragraph{Keep logical indices separate from physical addresses.}
The map \(b[j]\) does not imply that adjacent \(j\) values occupy
adjacent addresses. A view of a larger buffer can use a stride of
two, and a reversed view can use a negative stride if the source
permits it. Lower the logical lookup through the chosen layout:
start at the view's base and apply its stride. The fused loop can
select a contiguous fast path after guarding that layout, with a
general path for other views. In a test, interleave unrelated values
between the bias items and pass a view selecting only the intended
items. The results should still be \(25,43\). Checking only the
layout of newly allocated tensors would never exercise that path.
